!id combat-ai-roadmap-overview
Feature request for evolving the battle AI from a per-unit reactor into a
coordinated commander capable of executing timed tactics — against a single
human player, replaying the same battle repeatedly, at one of two difficulty
tiers.

The current AI is a reactor. Every decision cycle it re-reads the world,
picks targets, and issues orders — with only small amounts of persistence
(unit intent phases, repeat-attempt counters) surviving across cycles. This
produces competent local behavior but no tactics.

This roadmap adds, in order:

1. Four independent improvements that fix visible problems and build the
   vocabulary later work needs.
2. A plan layer — persistent, phased, role-assigning — that lets the AI
   commit to a sequence of actions across many decision cycles.
3. Tactics expressed as plans: retreat operations, combined-arms sequencing,
   hammer-and-anvil, then variations.

The ordering is deliberate and non-negotiable. Each stage supplies vocabulary
the next stage needs. Skipping ahead produces code that gets rewritten.

!id combat-ai-context
Confirmed from the codebase (CONTROLLABLE_TEAM_IDS = ['blue'], and the main
game constructs BattleAI for ['red'] only): single human (blue) vs. single
AI (red), no mixed-control teams. The human is expected to replay the same
battle — same map, same starting positions, same objective — repeatedly.
This shapes several design decisions:

- A deterministic plan layer on a fixed, repeated battle gets solved once
  and then loses the same way forever. Unpredictability is a hard
  requirement, not polish.

- There is no mixed-team scenario, so plan/player-order interaction is out
  of scope by construction, not deferred by assumption.

- Two difficulty tiers are required: Best-possible and Weakened. Both run
  identical machinery; only configuration differs.

!id combat-ai-design-taxonomy
Every behavioral feature falls into one of four buckets. Which bucket a
feature belongs to is decided by asking, in order: is this a one-shot
reaction to a threshold being crossed? If not: does the correct action
coincide with each unit's locally-optimal action?

AGREES — emergent, use local rules.
The unit maximizing its own outcome produces the coordinated behavior as a
side effect. Cheap, robust, already how most of the current AI works.
Examples already in the codebase:
- Flanking emerges from cavalry starting wide plus soft-target preference.
- Concentration emerges from weak-point bias with a detour limit.
- Kiting emerges from "archers back away from nearby charging enemies."
- Rout cascades emerge from allyRoutPenaltyPerTick.
- Screening emerges from a screen role plus a "don't pursue" rule.

DISAGREES — needs coordination.
The correct action requires one unit to know something about another unit's
state. No local rule can express this. General shapes:
- Timing relationships — "A then B." A local rule cannot say "wait."
- Deconfliction — "you go left, I go right." Locally both pick the closer.
- Sacrifice plays — "hold and die so I can flank."
- Deception — feigned retreat requires observing that the enemy pursues.

MIDDLE GROUND — light coordination.
Shared state without dictating action. A unit posts what it's doing; others
read and react. FocusFireCoordinator is this — a shared target everyone
reads. _archerInterceptOverride is this — archers broadcast a threat, line
units respond.

REFLEXES — fourth bucket.
Not local-emergent, not coordination, not a plan. A reflex is a one-shot,
event-triggered action outside the normal decision cycle: "I just crossed a
threshold, react now." A soldier at critical morale deciding to disengage is
a reflex, not a planning read. A4 (own-morale awareness) is a reflex wearing
a planning-read costume — the read itself is a reflex-trigger, even though
its consequences (exclusion from plan roles) reach into the plan layer.

The taxonomy drives the ordering below: reflexes and local rules first,
light coordination alongside, plans last. Doing it the other way produces a
plan layer built on vocabulary that hasn't been invented yet.

!id combat-ai-stage-a
STAGE A — INDEPENDENT IMPROVEMENTS

Do these first. Each fixes a visible problem, and each contributes vocabulary
the plan layer later consumes. None requires infrastructure the others need.

BASELINE SHIFT NOTE
Every acceptance criterion in Stages C and D that says "better than reactive
behavior" means reactive behavior AFTER Stage A (with line cohesion,
coordinated reinforcement, stable cavalry groups, and fragile-unit awareness
already in place). Re-derive those comparisons against the post-Stage-A
baseline, not the pre-Stage-A one.

A1. LINE COHESION
Problem: every line unit decides independently where to go. A fast unit
outruns its neighbors, arrives alone, gets flanked, and dies while its
neighbors watch from behind. The line dissolves into a cloud of individual
fights — the most common visual failure mode in playtest.

Desired behavior: a line unit's forward offset is capped relative to its
neighbors. If a neighbor is lagging, the advanced unit slows rather than
pressing ahead. When a neighbor is defeated and a gap opens, adjacent units
drift inward to close it. Mechanism: each line unit's forward limit is
derived from the median forward position of its neighbors on the line.
Neighbor sets are units sharing the same role and adjacent lateralOffset —
this makes line and cavalry neighbor sets disjoint by construction, so A1
and A3 never contend over the same positional logic.

Where it lives: LineBehavior.js for the forward-cap logic.
BattleLineFormation.js for exposing each unit's neighbors.

Acceptance criteria:
- In a battle with 6 line units per side, no line unit is more than ~2 ×
  lineUnitSpacing ahead of the median of its neighbors during the advance.
- When a front-line unit is defeated, the two units adjacent along the
  lateral axis visibly close the gap within a few seconds.
- The line arrives at contact as a line, not as a wedge or a scattered
  front.

Depends on: nothing.

A2. REINFORCEMENT REQUEST POOL
Problem: _reinforcementOverride asks "is any nearby ally being outnumbered?"
every cycle, for every unit. Three units can all pile onto the same needy
fight while a second one goes unattended. The algorithm also has no notion
of whether the fight is still winnable, whether this unit is the right type
to help, or whether anyone else is already going.

Desired behavior: a small coordinator that units post requests into, and
that resolves once per decision cycle with awareness of:
- Winnability. A fight already at 1:5 local odds is not worth sending help
  to — the helper dies too. Filter by a minimum ratio.
- Suitability. A spear unit is more valuable reinforcing against cavalry
  than against archers. Weight by attacker type vs. threatened ally type.
- Deconfliction. If three units would all be sent to the same fight, send
  one and route the others elsewhere. Track "already dispatched."

Where it lives: new file ReinforcementCoordinator.js. Consumed by
TeamAI._decide() in place of _reinforcementOverride.

Acceptance criteria:
- No two units are ever dispatched to the same threatened ally in the same
  decision cycle.
- A threatened unit at 1:5 local odds receives no reinforcement.
- A threatened unit at 1:1.5 local odds with a spear nearby and an archer
  threatened by cavalry receives the spear.

Depends on: nothing.

A3. CAVALRY FLANK LEADER
Problem: FlankerBehavior._preferSiblingTarget checks each sibling cavalry
unit individually. With two cavalry units this works. With three or more it
ping-pongs — A agrees with B, B with C, C with A — and the eventual shared
target depends on iteration order. Coordination is not stable.

Desired behavior: cavalry units on the same flank form a group with a
designated leader. The leader picks the target. Others follow unless they
have a strictly better local option (a routing enemy within chase range, for
instance). Group membership is determined by lateral position relative to
the battle line (left flank group, right flank group). The leader is chosen
deterministically (lowest unit id, or the unit furthest forward) so it is
stable tick to tick — no leader election thrashing.

Where it lives: new file CavalryGroup.js. TeamAI instantiates groups once
per decision cycle and passes them to FlankerBehavior in place of the
current siblingCavalry array.

Acceptance criteria:
- With 2, 3, or 4 cavalry units on the same side, target assignment is
  stable across decision cycles — no ping-pong between targets.
- All cavalry on a flank converge on the same target when the leader
  selects one, unless a unit has a routing-chase opportunity.
- Removing the leader (killed) causes a new leader to be selected within
  one decision cycle.

Depends on: nothing. Must land before the plan layer — the plan layer treats
a cavalry group as its hammer, and needs the group to exist as a stable
unit.

A4. OWN-MORALE AWARENESS (reflex-triggered)
Problem: BattleAssessment tracks team-average morale for posture, but the
planner has no per-unit morale awareness. A unit at morale 15 and one at
morale 95 look identical. A unit about to rout can still be assigned a
critical role — as the anvil in hammer-and-anvil, or as a flanking force —
and it will break the moment combat starts.

Desired behavior: the planner reads each unit's average morale when scoring.
A unit below a "fragile" threshold is:
- Excluded from critical plan roles (anvil, flanking reserve).
- Deprioritized for reinforcement dispatch.
- Given a defined fallback behavior. It does not simply sit idle.

Fragile-unit fallback behavior: a unit excluded from plan roles and
reinforcement dispatch must still do something coherent. Default: fall back
to a designated rally position behind the line (a "second line" / reserve
posture) and hold there, re-eligible for plan roles once morale recovers
above the fragile threshold. This must be implemented alongside A4 itself —
a fragile unit standing idle mid-battle reads as a bug, not a feature.

Where it lives: read function on Unit (or a helper in behaviorUtils.js).
Consumed by WinPlanner._scoreCandidate and by the Stage B plan layer's role
assignment. Fallback behavior lives alongside LineBehavior or as a small new
ReserveBehavior.

Acceptance criteria:
- A unit with average morale below 30 is never assigned as the anvil in a
  plan.
- A unit with average morale below 30 is never dispatched by the
  reinforcement coordinator.
- A fragile unit visibly moves to and holds a reserve position rather than
  continuing its previous order or standing idle in place.
- The HUD debug output (or an AI log line) shows the fragile-status read
  when the unit is evaluated.

Depends on: nothing.

!id combat-ai-stage-b
STAGE B — PLAN LAYER

This is the infrastructure. It does not itself produce visible behavior; it
enables Stage C and beyond. Done carefully, it composes Stage A's mechanisms;
done carelessly, it fights them.

B1. TEAM PLAN INFRASTRUCTURE
Problem: TeamAI._decide() is stateless. There is no concept of "we are
executing Plan X, currently at phase 2 of 4." Every cycle re-derives
everything from the current snapshot. Any behavior requiring timing — "wait
for the pin before charging," "bombard for three volleys, then charge" — is
inexpressible.

Desired behavior: a TeamPlan object persisted on TeamAI with:
- tactic — the tactic being executed ('hammer_and_anvil', 'retreat',
  'combined_arms', or 'none' for the default reactive mode).
- phase — a tactic-specific phase string.
- phaseStartedTick — for timeouts and duration checks.
- roles — a Map<unitId, role>. The anvil, the hammer, the screen, the
  reserve. Roles are how a plan says "you specifically do this."
- objectiveUnitId — the target of the tactic, or null.
- stagingPoint — a world position for hammer units to hold at, if the
  tactic uses one.

The plan is created, advanced, and dissolved by the plan layer. Behaviors
consult it (plan.phase === 'strike') but do not write to it.

Phase transitions are driven by conditions, not ticks:
- A phase defines an exitCondition — a predicate over world state.
- Every decision cycle, the plan layer checks the exit condition. When it
  holds, the plan advances to the next phase and resets phaseStartedTick.
- Every phase has a timeoutTicks. If the exit condition never holds within
  the timeout, the plan aborts back to 'none' and normal behavior resumes.

This is what makes plans robust rather than scripted. A hammer that never
gets its pin never charges; if the enemy never commits to the anvil, the
plan aborts and the units fall back to reactive behavior.

Role-map clearing on abort: units are not individually told "you are no
longer the anvil." The role map is simply dropped and units resume reactive
behavior next cycle. One tick of stale order is below perceptual threshold
and is accepted as-is. However, plan creation, phase transitions, and aborts
must all be visible in AIDebugLog starting in Stage B — not deferred to
Stage D — so that plan behavior is debuggable as soon as the plan layer
exists, before any tactic is built on top of it.

Plan priority and interruption: only one plan runs per team at a time.
Priority rules, in order:
1. Retreat is sticky and cannot be preempted. A team in a retreat plan does
   not switch to combined-arms or hammer-and-anvil mid-retreat, even if a
   good target of opportunity appears. A team assessed as needing to retreat
   has already been judged unable to win the current fight; letting a fresh
   target flip the plan reintroduces the "AI changes its mind every few
   seconds" failure the intent system exists to prevent.
2. Non-retreat plans (combined-arms, hammer-and-anvil) may only start when
   no plan is currently active. They do not preempt each other.
3. A plan's own abort conditions (objective destroyed, key unit destroyed,
   timeout) are the only way a non-retreat plan ends early.

Where it lives:
- TeamPlan.js — the plan object and its state machine.
- src/ai/plans/ — a directory of plan definitions. Each plan is a small
  module exporting { phases, assignRoles, entryCondition }.
- TeamAI gets a _currentPlan field, checked and advanced at the top of
  _decide() before any per-unit logic.
- PlanScheduler.js — a separate scheduler, invoked from _decide(), rather
  than trigger logic living inline. Simpler to test in isolation, and this
  is where B4's weighted plan selection lives.

Acceptance criteria:
- The plan layer can be observed via AIDebugLog — a new 'plan' category
  logging plan creation, phase transitions, and aborts.
- A plan with a never-satisfied exit condition aborts cleanly after its
  timeout and returns the team to reactive behavior.
- No behavior change in the default 'none' plan — the existing AI runs
  identically when no tactic is active.
- Retreat cannot be interrupted by another plan triggering; verified by a
  test that creates a strong combined-arms opportunity during an active
  retreat and confirms the retreat continues uninterrupted.

Depends on: nothing structurally, but Stage A's items should be complete
first so the plan layer consumes their interfaces rather than reinventing
them.

B2. PIN DETECTOR
Problem: "Is the enemy unit pinned?" is the central question for several
tactics. It needs a concrete, reliable answer.

Desired behavior: a pure function — not a system, not stateful — that takes
an enemy unit and returns { pinned: boolean, confidence: number }.

Pinned is defined as:
- A threshold fraction of the unit's living soldiers are in engaged or
  staggered state.
- The unit's center of mass has moved less than a threshold distance over
  the last N ticks.

Both conditions must hold. A unit that's fighting but maneuvering freely is
not pinned; a unit that's stationary but not engaged is not pinned either.

Threshold specification: the engaged-fraction threshold cannot be a single
global constant — a 5-wide line unit's engaged fraction looks nothing like a
3-wide archer unit's under identical tactical conditions. Required:
- Threshold is per unit-type (line, archer, cavalry, etc.), not global.
  Store as a lookup in AIConfig, not a single scalar.
- Movement threshold (center-of-mass drift) is similarly per-type — a
  cavalry unit's "stationary" baseline jitter is not a line unit's.
- Exploit resistance, on the Best-possible tier specifically: a human player
  will deliberately probe the pin condition — holding a unit in apparent
  engagement without committing, to bait a hammer charge into a trap. The
  fix is not to hide or obscure the thresholds (a hidden constant is still a
  constant once found). The fix is to require the pinned condition to hold
  continuously for a minimum duration before it's trusted, so that faking a
  pin costs the player real time they could have spent elsewhere. This
  duration is itself a tier-config parameter.

Where it lives: PinDetector.js. Consumed by plan exit conditions and by the
hammer-and-anvil tactic. Thresholds in AIConfig, keyed by unit type.

Acceptance criteria:
- Correctly identifies an enemy line unit locked in melee as pinned.
- Correctly identifies a maneuvering enemy as not pinned, even if some of
  its soldiers are engaged.
- Correctly identifies a stationary but disengaged enemy as not pinned.
- Thresholds are in AIConfig, per unit type, so they can be tuned without
  touching code.
- On Best-possible, a unit that fakes engagement for less than the minimum
  hold duration does not register as pinned.

Depends on: unit center-tracking history — a small ring buffer per unit
added in Stage B or supplied by Unit.

B3. PLAN / ACTION COST-RISK GATE
Problem: a plan that succeeds 70% of the time and fails catastrophically the
other 30% is worse than reactive behavior, because the failures are what the
player remembers. Hammer-and-anvil that lands clean two out of three times
and throws a cavalry unit into a braced spear wall the other time produces a
worse outcome than an AI that never attempted the charge. "Will it land?"
(feasibility — the existing entry/exit conditions) is necessary but not
sufficient. "Is it worth the risk if it doesn't land clean?" is a separate
question and needs its own gate.

Desired behavior: before a plan (or any weighted candidate under B4) is
eligible for selection, it passes an expected-value estimate: projected
benefit if it succeeds, weighted against projected cost if it fails, using a
conservative prior derived from current world state (unit counts, morale,
terrain-blind since terrain is out of scope). Because the opponent is a
human and does not behave like a fixed AI, this cannot be tuned against
opponent history — it uses a conservative prior ("assume average-case enemy
response") rather than an adaptive opponent model. No cross-battle or
within-battle player modeling is used anywhere in this system.

Where it lives: PlanRiskGate.js, consulted by PlanScheduler (B1) before a
plan is added to the weighted candidate pool (B4).

Acceptance criteria:
- A plan whose projected downside (e.g., losing the hammer group) exceeds a
  configured risk tolerance is excluded from the candidate pool, even if its
  upside is high.
- Risk tolerance is a tier-config parameter (Best-possible tolerates more
  risk than Weakened).
- Verified via a scripted scenario where the only available charge target is
  heavily defended: the plan is excluded, not merely down-weighted.

Depends on: B1 (plan layer).

B4. WEIGHTED PLAN / ACTION SELECTION (unpredictability)
Problem: a battle that is replayed identically must not produce an AI that
is a pure deterministic function of the situation. If the AI always picks
the single best-scored plan or role assignment for a given world state, a
player who replays the same opening will eventually see the same AI response
every time and can solve it permanently.

This is explicitly not solved by reading or remembering the player. It is
solved by the AI itself being non-deterministic at decision time,
independent of anything about the player.

Desired behavior: at every decision point that currently selects "the best
X" — plan selection, cavalry group targeting, reinforcement dispatch,
refused-flank side selection, hammer-and-anvil role assignment, and any
future equivalent — replace deterministic argmax selection with:
1. Score all viable candidates (plans, targets, roles) using existing
   scoring logic plus the B3 risk gate.
2. Exclude any candidate below a quality floor — a threshold defined as
   "within X% of the top-scored candidate," not an absolute score. This is
   what keeps "sometimes suboptimal" from becoming "sometimes bad." The
   floor width (X%) is a tier-config parameter.
3. Weighted-random sample among the surviving candidates, weighted toward
   higher scores (e.g., softmax over score, temperature as a tier-config
   parameter). The top candidate is picked most often; other good candidates
   are picked sometimes.

This applies everywhere the codebase currently has a "pick the best one"
step, not only at the top-level plan scheduler — this is a wider change than
B1-B3 alone and touches A2 (reinforcement dispatch), A3 (cavalry target
selection among ties), C3/D1 (role assignment, refused-flank side), and the
plan scheduler itself.

Selection is made fresh at each decision point using only current world
state — no memory of prior battles, no memory of earlier in this battle
beyond what normal world-state reads already provide.

Where it lives: a shared utility, WeightedSelect.js, used by PlanScheduler
(B1) and retrofitted into A2, A3, C3, and D1's existing selection steps.

Acceptance criteria:
- Replaying an identical battle opening multiple times produces observably
  different AI plan choices and role assignments across replays, without any
  change in world state inputs.
- No selection ever picks a candidate outside the quality floor — verified
  by asserting the selected candidate's score against the top score in a
  scripted test.
- On Weakened, the quality floor is wider (more permissive) than on
  Best-possible, so weaker-but-still-technically-eligible options are chosen
  more often — this is part of what makes Weakened play worse, on top of the
  other tier parameters, not a separate "deliberately bad" behavior.

Depends on: B1 (plan layer), B3 (risk gate feeds the candidate pool).

!id combat-ai-tier-configuration
TIER CONFIGURATION

Two named presets, not a slider: best_possible and weakened. Both run
identical plan-layer machinery, identical behaviors, identical selection
logic (B4). Only configuration differs. This is the intended way to build
difficulty — not by making the AI decide badly, but by dialing the same
decision-making back.

Parameters:
- Fog of war: Best-possible OFF (full information), Weakened OFF.
- Plan pool: Best-possible all plans eligible, Weakened subset of plans.
- Quality floor (B4): Best-possible narrow (only near-top candidates
  eligible), Weakened wide (mediocre candidates sometimes chosen).
- Risk tolerance (B3): Best-possible higher (accepts riskier plans for
  higher upside), Weakened lower (avoids risk aggressively).
- Abort thresholds: Best-possible tight (commits only when a plan will
  likely land), Weakened loose (aborts easily, commits less).
- Reaction delay: Best-possible 0 ticks, Weakened N ticks (waits before
  responding to new threats).
- PinDetector minimum hold duration: Best-possible present, tuned to cost
  the player meaningfully; Weakened shorter or absent.
- Concentration aggressiveness: Best-possible high, Weakened low.
- ChargeReadiness bar: Best-possible permissive, Weakened conservative.
- Telegraphing: Best-possible none — the AI owes the player no legibility
  on this tier. Weakened may be present as an intentional design choice —
  visible tells before committing to a charge, readable pauses, etc.

On full information: Best-possible uses full battlefield awareness — the
existing non-goal ("the AI sees the whole battlefield, fog of war is out of
scope") applies to both tiers as a baseline, but on Best-possible this is
also load-bearing for what "best possible" means: an omniscient opponent,
not merely a smarter one. This should be a named, stated design decision in
any player-facing difficulty description, not discovered by the player as an
implicit asymmetry. If a "no full-information" Best-possible tier is wanted
later, that's a third config preset, not a rewrite of this system.

On telegraphing: Best-possible does not telegraph because it has no
obligation to be a fair or legible opponent — its only goal is to be the
hardest fight the AI can produce, and unpredictability (B4) rather than
readability is what keeps it interesting across replays. Weakened may
telegraph as one of several parameters that make it feel weaker without the
AI "playing dumb" in kind — it is still smart, just easier to read and more
forgiving.

!id combat-ai-stage-c
STAGE C — BEHAVIORS EXPRESSED AS PLANS

Now that the plan layer exists, the items that were waiting for it can be
built. All acceptance criteria below are validated against the post-Stage-A
baseline and against multiple replays of the same battle, not a single
playthrough — per B4, a single playthrough cannot demonstrate that plan
selection is actually varying.

C1. RETREAT OPERATION
Problem: posture 'retreat' currently returns null from LineBehavior, meaning
TeamAI issues no new order. The unit stands where it is, in contact with the
enemy, and is ground down. There is no concept of "withdrawing to a specific
place" — only "moving to a specific place."

Desired behavior: retreat is a multi-phase plan.
Phase Disengage: units not currently in melee pull back toward a rally line.
Engaged units hold. Exit condition: all non-engaged units are behind the
rally line.
Phase Screen: a designated rear-guard unit holds at the rally line, facing
the enemy. Exit condition: rear-guard unit is in position.
Phase Reform: all units rally at the line, re-form facing the enemy. Exit
condition: no unit is currently routing.
Phase Reassess: plan dissolves; normal behavior resumes with fresh
assessment.

If the reassessment still reads 'retreat', a fresh retreat plan is created
with a new rally line further back. Retreat continues in waves rather than
one deep withdrawal. Per B1's priority rule, this plan is sticky and cannot
be preempted by combined-arms or hammer-and-anvil while active.

Where it lives: src/ai/plans/RetreatPlan.js. Triggered from TeamAI._decide()
when assessment.posture === 'retreat' and no plan is currently active.

Acceptance criteria:
- A team losing badly visibly withdraws to a defensible line rather than
  standing in place and dying.
- Engaged units are not yanked out of melee — the retreat starts with
  unengaged units.
- If the enemy doesn't pursue, the retreat stabilizes at the rally line and
  the team reforms.
- A fresh combined-arms opportunity appearing mid-retreat does not interrupt
  the retreat (verifies B1's priority rule).

Depends on: B1 (plan layer).

C2. COMBINED-ARMS SEQUENCING
Problem: archers fire, cavalry charges, and infantry advances all happen in
parallel, on independent clocks. Real combined arms is about sequence:
soften with arrows, charge the softened target, advance through the gap.

Desired behavior: a combined-arms plan applied to a single objective unit.
Phase Bombard: all archers focus-fire the objective. Infantry hold at
standoff. Cavalry hold at staging. Exit condition: objective at or below a
threshold HP fraction, or N volleys fired, or timeout.
Phase Charge: cavalry charges the objective (using ChargeReadiness).
Infantry advance to contact. Exit condition: cavalry is engaged with the
objective, or charge aborted.
Phase Exploit: all units press the objective; whatever survives charges
through. Exit condition: objective destroyed, or timeout.

Key timing property: the charge phase does not begin until the bombard
phase's exit condition holds. If the objective is not softened, the charge
is delayed — and if the bombard times out without softening, the plan aborts
to reactive behavior rather than charging into a fresh target.

Where it lives: src/ai/plans/CombinedArmsPlan.js. Triggered from
PlanScheduler when the WinPlanner objective exists and the team is in
'press' or 'hold' posture (not retreat), subject to B4's weighted selection
against other viable plans and B3's risk gate.

Acceptance criteria:
- Archers fire first; cavalry does not commit until archer fire has landed.
- If the objective never softens (archers blocked, or the objective is
  shielded and regenerating), the plan aborts without a charge.
- Against the post-Stage-A reactive AI, combined-arms sequencing visibly
  produces better outcomes than the same units without sequencing.

Depends on: B1 (plan layer), B3 (risk gate), B4 (weighted selection against
hammer-and-anvil when both are viable), A3 (cavalry group as the charge
force). B2 (PinDetector) not needed here — the exit condition is HP-based,
not pin-based.

C3. POSTURE CONSUMPTION
Problem: press and hold postures are computed and never read. Only 'retreat'
has any effect (and even that is weak, per C1).

Desired behavior:
- Press loosens the line-concentration detour limit, widens the focus-fire
  preference toward aggressive targets, and allows cavalry to accept riskier
  charges (a lower ChargeReadiness bar).
- Hold changes nothing.
- Retreat triggers the retreat plan (C1).

Where posture-conditional parameters offer multiple viable choices (e.g.,
which aggressive target to concentrate on under press), selection uses B4's
weighted approach rather than always picking the single best target.

Where it lives: AIConfig gains posture-conditional parameters; TeamAI._decide()
selects the parameter set based on the current posture before passing them to
behaviors.

Acceptance criteria:
- A team with press posture visibly concentrates harder on one point than
  the same team with hold posture.
- Posture changes do not cause thrashing — a team flipping press/hold/press
  on consecutive cycles doesn't oscillate its orders.

Depends on: B1 (plan layer for the retreat half).

!id combat-ai-stage-d
STAGE D — HAMMER-AND-ANVIL

The first concrete tactic. Exercises every part of the plan layer, and is
the visible payoff for Stages A-C.

D1. HAMMER-AND-ANVIL TACTIC
Problem: the AI has no way to execute a timing-dependent tactical
combination. Each arm acts independently; cavalry charges whatever passes
readiness, whenever it passes readiness.

Desired behavior: a plan with phases.
Phase Advance — Anvil (line units): advance to contact with the objective.
Hammer (cavalry group): move to a staging point off the objective's flank,
out of contact range. Exit: anvil within engagement range of objective.
Phase Pin — Anvil: fight the objective. Do not pursue if it retreats.
Hammer: hold at staging. Exit: PinDetector says the objective is pinned
(including minimum hold duration on Best-possible — see B2).
Phase Strike — Anvil: continue fighting, hold the objective's attention.
Hammer: charge the objective's flank or rear. Exit: hammer makes contact,
or charge aborts.
Phase Exploit — Anvil: press through. Hammer: regroup and re-engage. Exit:
objective destroyed, or timeout.

Role assignment:
- Anvil — the line units nearest the objective, capped at a number
  proportional to the objective's size (roughly 1.5 anvil units per enemy
  line unit engaged). Fragile units (A4) are excluded and instead follow
  A4's defined fallback behavior.
- Hammer — the cavalry group on the flank nearer the objective (A3). If no
  cavalry is available, the plan aborts — no cavalry, no hammer.
- Everyone else — reactive, in case the tactic fails and a broader fight
  develops.

Where more than one objective or more than one cavalry group is a viable
hammer, B4's weighted selection picks among good options rather than always
the single best-scored pairing.

Abort conditions: the plan aborts if:
- The objective dies (success — dissolves cleanly).
- The anvil is destroyed.
- The hammer is destroyed.
- The PinDetector never reports pinned within the plan's total timeout.
- Any phase's individual timeout is exceeded.
- B3's risk gate would no longer justify the plan given a significant
  change in world state since entry (e.g., the objective gained
  reinforcements).

On abort, the plan returns the team to reactive behavior and clears the role
map — the units that were anvils and hammers become normal units again.

Where it lives: src/ai/plans/HammerAndAnvilPlan.js. Triggered from
PlanScheduler when the WinPlanner objective is a good candidate and cavalry
is available and the team is in a 'press' or 'hold' posture, subject to B3's
risk gate and B4's weighted selection against combined-arms.

Acceptance criteria:
- A visibly better outcome than the same units fighting reactively (against
  the post-Stage-A baseline) — the charge lands on a pinned unit rather than
  a maneuvering one.
- If the enemy refuses to engage (never gets pinned), the plan aborts
  cleanly without a wasted charge.
- If the enemy reacts by turning to face the hammer, the plan is not
  defeated by this alone — the charge may still land clean, or may abort,
  but the tactic does not collapse into confusion.
- Across multiple replays of the same battle with the same player opening,
  the AI does not always choose hammer-and-anvil over combined-arms (or vice
  versa) when both are viable — demonstrates B4 is actually active at the
  tactic-selection level, not just within a single tactic's role assignment.

Depends on: B1 (plan layer), B2 (PinDetector), B3 (risk gate), B4 (weighted
selection), A3 (cavalry group), A4 (fragile exclusion and fallback).

!id combat-ai-stage-e
STAGE E — TACTIC VARIATIONS

Cheaper once Stage D exists. Each is a variation on the plan machinery.

E1. REFUSED FLANK
One end of the line sits further back so the enemy stretches to reach it and
exposes their own flank. The vocabulary exists (depthOffset); the missing
piece is a reason to refuse a flank, which the posture system supplies.

Triggered when the team is locally weaker on one side. The plan assigns a
deeper depthOffset to the weak-side units and shifts the concentration
objective toward the strong side. Side selection (left vs. right, when both
are plausible) goes through B4's weighted selection rather than always
picking the single best-scored side.

Depends on: B1, B4, C3.

E2. REFUSED CENTER
Same idea, applied to the middle of the line. Requires non-straight line
geometry, which BattleLineFormation does not currently produce. Moderate
work — the formation needs a "bowed line" mode.

Depends on: B1, E1 (as a template).

E3. DOUBLE ENVELOPMENT
Both flanks wrap simultaneously. Two hammer groups, two objectives, or one
objective attacked from two sides.

Depends on: B1, A3 (for multiple cavalry groups), D1 (as a template).

!id combat-ai-stage-f
STAGE F — ADVANCED TACTICS

Last. Each has a specific additional requirement.

F1. FEIGNED RETREAT
Pull back to draw pursuers, then counterattack into them.

Additional requirement: a "combat-ready withdrawal" state, distinct from
current retreat. Units maintain formation and facing while withdrawing, and
retain the ability to turn and fight. This is a soldier-state-machine
addition, not a plan-layer concern.

On Best-possible, this tactic is not obligated to telegraph or be fair —
consistent with the tier table above. Its robustness against a player who
learns to recognize it across replays comes from B4: the AI does not always
open with, or always commit to, a feigned retreat under the same conditions,
so the player cannot rely on "the AI always fakes here." On Weakened, this
tactic may be visibly telegraphed or simply excluded from the plan pool
entirely, consistent with the tier table.

Depends on: B1, C1, B4, soldier state-machine extension.

F2. ECHELON ATTACK
Commit units in sequence, rotate tired units out. Needs unit-level stamina
(soldier fatigue exists; unit-level aggregation does not) and a rotation
schedule.

Depends on: B1, unit-level stamina tracking.

!id combat-ai-non-goals
NON-GOALS

- Perfect information (fog of war). The AI will continue to see the whole
  battlefield on both tiers. Scouting and fog of war are out of scope. (Note
  this is reinforced as an explicit choice on Best-possible; see the tier
  config entry.)

- Terrain awareness. The AI treats the field as a blank plane. If terrain is
  added later, this roadmap is a prerequisite but not a solution.

- Player-side AI. The player-controlled team is not driven by any of this.

- Micro-level optimization. This roadmap is about tactics, not about making
  individual soldiers fight better. Soldier-level combat is unchanged.

- Any opponent modeling, within-battle or cross-battle. Explicitly and
  deliberately excluded. The AI does not read player tendencies during a
  battle and does not persist any profile of the player between battles.
  Unpredictability across replays is achieved entirely through B4's weighted
  selection at decision time, not through adapting to the player. This was
  seriously considered and rejected during design review: it adds substantial
  complexity (a bad opponent model is worse than none) and isn't what's
  wanted — the AI should always plan smart and react properly to the actual
  current world state, and separately, sometimes choose a good-but-not-
  optimal action for unpredictability's sake. Those are the only two things
  going on.

- Mixed human/AI teams and player-order/plan interaction. Out of scope by
  construction — confirmed single-human-vs-single-AI, no mixed-control teams
  exist. Not deferred by assumption; deferred because the scenario doesn't
  exist in this game.

- A third, continuous difficulty slider. Two named presets only. Do not
  build a generalized difficulty-parameter slider system; it isn't needed
  and adds complexity the two-preset system doesn't have.

!id combat-ai-ordering-rationale
ORDERING RATIONALE

Every stage depends on the ones before it, and the dependency is not
optional. In summary:

- Stage A supplies vocabulary: a stable cavalry group, a coordinated
  reinforcement pattern, a fragile-unit read (and its fallback behavior).
  The plan layer consumes all of it.

- Stage B supplies the infrastructure: persistent plans, phase state
  machines, role assignment, a pin detector, a cost-risk gate, and weighted
  unpredictable selection.

- Stage C uses Stage B to express the behaviors that were waiting for it:
  retreat, combined-arms, posture consumption — validated against the
  post-Stage-A baseline and across multiple replays.

- Stage D is the first real tactic — the visible payoff.

- Stage E and F are variations, each cheaper than the last because the
  machinery exists.

Doing stages out of order produces code that gets rewritten. Building the
plan layer before A produces a role system that doesn't fit the coordination
patterns Stage A establishes. Building tactics before B means hardcoding the
same sequence multiple times. Building B4 (weighted selection) after D
instead of within B means retrofitting non-determinism into a tactic that was
designed and tested deterministic — significantly more expensive than
building it in from the start.

!id combat-ai-resolved-questions
RESOLVED QUESTIONS (previously open)

1. Where does the plan layer's trigger logic live?
   Resolved: a separate PlanScheduler, invoked from _decide(). More testable
   than an inline pre-pass, and it's the natural home for B4's weighted
   selection.

2. How are roles cleared on abort?
   Resolved: the role map is dropped; units resume reactive behavior next
   cycle. One tick of stale order is accepted as invisible. Plan events are
   logged from Stage B onward so this is debuggable early.

3. Does the plan layer interact with UnitIntent?
   Resolved: kept separate. Intent is per-unit persistence; plan is team-
   level persistence. A plan assigns the cavalry group a role; the group's
   internal intents still govern individual unit behavior within that role.

4. What happens if two plans want the same unit?
   Resolved: only one plan runs per team at a time. Retreat is sticky and
   cannot be preempted. Non-retreat plans do not preempt each other and only
   end via their own abort conditions.

5. How is plan success measured?
   Resolved: each plan defines its own success condition, logged at plan
   dissolution (objective died for hammer-and-anvil; no units died during
   the retreat for C1).

6. Does opponent modeling belong in this system, at any timescale?
   Resolved, after review: no. Neither within-battle nor cross-battle
   player modeling is built. Unpredictability comes entirely from B4.

7. Player-order / plan interaction for mixed-control teams?
   Resolved: out of scope by construction — no mixed-control teams exist in
   this game (single human vs. single AI).

!id combat-ai-change-log
CHANGE LOG

v1. Initial version. Covers Stages A-F, with the emergence/plan taxonomy as
the guiding design principle.

v2. Added: reflexes as a fourth taxonomy bucket; fragile-unit fallback
behavior (A4); PinDetector per-unit-type thresholds and exploit-resistance
via minimum hold duration (B2); plan priority rules with retreat stickiness
(B1); plan-event logging moved to Stage B (B1); a plan/action cost-risk gate
(B3, new); weighted, unpredictable plan and role selection to prevent the AI
being solved across repeated replays of the same battle (B4, new); a
two-tier (best_possible / weakened) configuration system replacing a
continuous-difficulty assumption; explicit rejection of any opponent-
modeling approach (within-battle or cross-battle) as a non-goal, in favor of
B4's decision-time unpredictability; baseline-shift note for Stage C/D
acceptance criteria; explicit scope note on the human-vs-AI, repeated-battle
context driving these changes.

